I used a small phone keypad array and a recursive helper. At each position, the helper tries every letter for the current digit. Once the path has one letter for every digit, it is added to the answer.

For `digits = "23"`, the first level tries `a`, `b`, and `c`. After choosing `a`, the next level adds `d`, `e`, and `f`, giving `ad`, `ae`, and `af`. Then the same work is done for `b` and `c`.

The empty input is handled before starting the recursion. The input string is not changed.

```cpp
class Solution {
private:
    void build(const string& digits, int index, string& current,
               vector<string>& result, const vector<string>& phone) {
        if (index == static_cast<int>(digits.size())) {
            result.push_back(current);
            return;
        }

        const string& letters = phone[digits[index] - '0'];
        for (char letter : letters) {
            current.push_back(letter);
            build(digits, index + 1, current, result, phone);
            current.pop_back();
        }
    }

public:
    vector<string> letterCombinations(string digits) {
        if (digits.empty()) {
            return {};
        }

        vector<string> phone = {
            "", "", "abc", "def", "ghi",
            "jkl", "mno", "pqrs", "tuv", "wxyz"
        };

        vector<string> result;
        string current;
        build(digits, 0, current, result, phone);
        return result;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n * 4^n)` in the worst case, including the cost of storing each string.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the current path and recursion stack, not counting the returned result.

A queue makes the levels easy to see. Start with an empty string. For each digit, remove every string from the current level and add all letters belonging to that digit. The new strings form the next level.

For example, with `"27"`, the first level becomes `a`, `b`, `c`. Each of those is then expanded with `p`, `q`, `r`, and `s`.

The queue only contains strings that still need processing or are already complete. An empty input returns an empty list.

```java
class Solution {
    public List<String> letterCombinations(String digits) {
        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (digits == null || digits.isEmpty()) {
            return result;
        }

        String[] phone = {
            "", "", "abc", "def", "ghi",
            "jkl", "mno", "pqrs", "tuv", "wxyz"
        };

        Queue<String> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer("");

        for (int i = 0; i < digits.length(); i++) {
            String letters = phone[digits.charAt(i) - '0'];
            int levelSize = queue.size();

            for (int j = 0; j < levelSize; j++) {
                String prefix = queue.poll();
                for (char letter : letters.toCharArray()) {
                    queue.offer(prefix + letter);
                }
            }
        }

        result.addAll(queue);
        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n * 4^n)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(n * 4^n)` for the strings held by the queue and the returned list.

Here the helper receives the part of the digit string that is still left. It takes one letter from the first remaining digit and calls itself with the rest. When no digits remain, the built string is complete.

The important edge cases are `null` and `""`, both of which return an empty list. Digits `7` and `9` work without any special branch because their mappings simply contain four letters.

```java
class Solution {
    private final String[] phone = {
        "", "", "abc", "def", "ghi",
        "jkl", "mno", "pqrs", "tuv", "wxyz"
    };

    public List<String> letterCombinations(String digits) {
        List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();
        if (digits == null || digits.isEmpty()) {
            return result;
        }

        generate(digits, 0, new StringBuilder(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private void generate(String digits, int index, StringBuilder current,
                          List<String> result) {
        if (index == digits.length()) {
            result.add(current.toString());
            return;
        }

        String letters = phone[digits.charAt(index) - '0'];
        for (int i = 0; i < letters.length(); i++) {
            current.append(letters.charAt(i));
            generate(digits, index + 1, current, result);
            current.deleteCharAt(current.length() - 1);
        }
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n * 4^n)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack and the reusable `StringBuilder`, excluding returned combinations.

Instead of building a tree, this version treats each answer as a number written with a different base at each position. A digit with three letters has base 3, while `7` and `9` have base 4.

For `"23"`, there are `3 * 3 = 9` possible indexes. Index `0` maps to `ad`, index `1` to `ae`, and so on. The code converts each index into one letter choice per digit.

This is a little less natural than backtracking, but it avoids a recursive helper. The input is read only, not modified.

```csharp
public class Solution
{
    public IList<string> LetterCombinations(string digits)
    {
        var result = new List<string>();
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(digits))
        {
            return result;
        }

        string[] phone = {
            "", "", "abc", "def", "ghi",
            "jkl", "mno", "pqrs", "tuv", "wxyz"
        };

        long total = 1;
        foreach (char digit in digits)
        {
            total *= phone[digit - '0'].Length;
        }

        for (long number = 0; number < total; number++)
        {
            long value = number;
            char[] combination = new char[digits.Length];

            for (int index = digits.Length - 1; index >= 0; index--)
            {
                string letters = phone[digits[index] - '0'];
                combination[index] = letters[(int)(value % letters.Length)];
                value /= letters.Length;
            }

            result.Add(new string(combination));
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n * 4^n)` in the worst case.
Space complexity: `O(n)` temporary space per combination, excluding the result list.

